Set this machine up to run olr, and start it.

Copies this binary to /usr/local/bin if it is not already on a
system path, writes the systemd units to /lib/systemd/system naming
wherever the binary ended up, creates /etc/open-linux-router/olrd.env if it
does not exist, corrects the units' paths when dnsmasq or unbound live
somewhere other than Debian puts them, and enables the service so it
survives a reboot.

Only the control plane is enabled. No DHCP server appears and no
resolver takes over port 53 — each module enables its own backend
when you configure it.

Everything written is printed. Use --dry-run to see it first.

Usage:
  olr enable [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for enable

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
